        "Part I — The Core Method",
        "1.1 The common way people use AI\nA typical interaction looks like this:\n- “Write a viral post.”\n- “Create a business plan.”\n- “Make me a YouTube script.”\n- “Give me a marketing strategy.”\n- “Tell me how to get fit.”\nThe user gives a broad request, AI fills in the missing details, and the user judges the result.",
        "The problem is that the AI has to guess:\n- What quality means in this situation.\n- Which method should be followed.\n- What the user already knows.\n- What constraints apply.\n- Which style or format is appropriate.\n- What the user is trying to achieve.\n- Which details matter most.\nWhen those details are missing, the model falls back on generic patterns. The answer may be plausible and reasonably polished, but it is unlikely to reflect the user's exact standards or situation.",
        "This is why repeated prompt guessing often produces a series of slightly different generic answers rather than a dependable result.\n1.2 Think of AI as a digital employee\nThe source recommends thinking of AI as a digital employee. The metaphor is useful because an employee needs more than a job title.",
        "If you hired someone and said, “Make my business successful,” you would not expect them to understand your customers, product, quality standards, brand, budget, or preferred process automatically.",
        "You would need to explain:\n- The outcome you want.\n- The task they are responsible for.\n- The method they should follow.\n- The information they need.\n- The standards they must meet.\n- The constraints they must respect.\n- How the result should be delivered.\nAI is similar. It can perform many useful tasks, but the quality of the result depends heavily on the clarity of the instructions and the context supplied.\n1.3 The quality gap: generic output versus directed output\nConsider a request to create a YouTube script about productivity.",
        "A generic prompt might say:\nWrite a viral YouTube script about productivity.",
