        "The aim is not simply to get a better answer once. It is to create a process that consistently produces better starting points and helps you improve your own judgment.\n1.4 Why detailed instructions matter\nThe source recommends substantial prompts—sometimes hundreds or even thousands of words—for complex tasks. The exact length is not the goal. Completeness is.",
        "A short prompt can be sufficient for a simple request. A complex task may require detailed instructions covering:\n- Role and objective.\n- Method or framework.\n- Required inputs.\n- Sequence of steps.\n- Decision rules.\n- Quality criteria.\n- Constraints and exclusions.\n- Output format.\n- Feedback and revision process.\nThe more consequential or specialised the task, the more important it is to define what good work looks like.",
        "Do not make prompts long for the sake of length. Make them precise enough that the model does not have to invent important requirements.\n1.5 Taste is part of the instruction\nThe source describes the goal as moving from generic AI output to output shaped by your own sense of taste.",
