        "5.1 Why ordinary question-and-answer is not enough\nWhen learning a difficult subject, a basic AI answer may provide a quick explanation but not necessarily a deep understanding.",
        "The source argues that people are shaped by the thinkers they learn from. It therefore recommends building a reference library of important thinkers' worldviews, principles, and reasoning methods.",
        "The purpose is not to make AI impersonate a person or claim to reproduce their mind. It is to study their publicly available ideas and use those ideas as lenses for examining problems.\n5.2 Build a worldview reference document\nChoose a thinker, researcher, author, or practitioner whose ideas you want to understand.",
        "Use reliable source material such as:\n- Books or excerpts you are entitled to use.\n- Essays and articles.\n- Interviews and lectures.\n- Public talks.\n- Research papers.\n- Long-form discussions.\nAsk AI to create a structured guide covering:\n1. Core principles and recurring ideas.\n2. How the person approaches problems.\n3. Important concepts and distinctions.\n4. Major arguments or discoveries.\n5. Examples that illustrate the ideas.\n6. Questions or problems the framework is useful for.\n7. Areas where the person's views have changed or are contested, if the sources establish this.\n8. Important limitations or disagreements.\nA useful instruction:\nCreate a comprehensive study guide to the ideas and problem-solving\napproach of [thinker or author], based on the sources I provide or\nreliable sources you can access.",
