        "6.1 Treat thinking as a process you can practise\nThe source argues that strong writers and creators often use repeatable ways of thinking. Rather than waiting for inspiration, they ask structured questions that help them develop an idea.",
        "A creative thought partner can turn that process into an interactive routine.\n6.2 The source's idea-development questions\nThe source describes a sequence of questions used when developing an outline:\n1. What is the big problem related to this topic?\n2. What happens if the problem is not solved?\n3. What would the ideal situation look like?\n4. What unusual concepts, perspectives, or personal experiences could shed new light on the topic?\n5. What practical step-by-step process could help someone move toward the ideal situation?\n6. What relevant quotes, anecdotes, studies, or statistics could strengthen the argument?\nThese questions can help move from a broad topic to a more developed collection of ideas.",
        "Do not add quotations, studies, or statistics merely to make an argument look authoritative. Verify them and make sure they genuinely support the point.\n6.3 Build a guided thinking prompt\nThe source recommends using a thinking method—such as first-principles questioning—as the instructions for a thought-partner prompt.",
